// A worn track: the same soil as the meadow floor, seen in the open.
//
// ## Where these colours come from
//
// Sampled off `docs/references/grass_to_mud_bumpy.jpg`, eight stops from shadow
// to highlight, in linear light. The endpoints are the extremes of the plate,
// not percentiles of it:
//
//     shadow        0.045, 0.028, 0.016
//     midpoint      0.106, 0.067, 0.038
//     highlight     0.210, 0.132, 0.076
//
// The ratios matter more than the levels and they hold across the whole range:
// **G/R about 0.63, B/R about 0.36.** An earlier ramp sat at G/R of nearly one,
// which is why a whole track of it read as sand — the error was the green, never
// the brightness.
//
// The midpoint is brighter than the plate's measured median of 0.084 on purpose.
// `grain_strength` multiplies a noise into this palette downstream, which costs
// roughly a quarter of the light, so a palette calibrated *to* the median
// renders below it. These numbers are calibrated to what comes out.
(
    format: "ground-material",
    format_version: 1,
    material: (
        key: "compacted_loam",
        display_name: "Compacted loam",

        optics: (
            dry_palette: (
                // Cooled and desaturated, and the reason is measured rather
                // than tasteful. Rendered beside the grass, the old stops came
                // back at a red-to-blue ratio of 2.93 against the grass's 1.84
                // — a broad orange midtone behind dark green geometry, which
                // reads as two different worlds however well the boundary is
                // blended. These land near 1.95 at the same luminance.
                // ## Recalibrated, once the tonal ramp started working
                //
                // `tone_norm`'s input socket was a disabled duplicate for the
                // whole of this profile's life, so the palette ramp ran at a
                // constant and every soil rendered at its *low* stop. These
                // numbers were authored against that, which is why the track
                // came out at two and a half times the grass's luminance the
                // moment the socket was fixed — the same palette, finally
                // showing its middle.
                //
                // Calibrated against the render rather than scaled by a factor,
                // which is why these are not simply the old numbers halved — an
                // earlier revision of this comment said "halved" and the stops
                // had in fact risen about five per cent from their parent. The
                // measurement that fixed them is the track's luminance against
                // the grass beside it: 1.10x in the approved plate, 2.88x when
                // the ramp first started working, and these are what bring it
                // back.
                //
                // Warmed to a red-to-blue ratio of 2.70. The cooling pass took
                // it to 2.00 to sit in the grass's world and rendered at 1.44
                // against the grass's 1.84 — cooler than the thing it was
                // supposed to match.
                //
                // ## Halved, and browned, against the reference's own ratio
                //
                // The measurement that settles this needs no exposure match,
                // because the reference photograph contains **both** the grass
                // and the mud. Its mud runs at **0.90 times** the mean luminance
                // of the turf beside it — bare earth is very slightly darker
                // than a green sward, which is the opposite of what everybody
                // expects and is why this kept drifting upward.
                //
                // Rendered on the comparison card these stops came back at
                // **1.79**. Twice the reference's relationship, which is a pale
                // beige card sitting next to dark green geometry, and it is what
                // "khaki" and "looks nothing like dirt" were describing.
                //
                // The hue had drifted too, and from this file's own stated
                // target: the header says G/R about 0.63 and every stop here was
                // at **0.76**. Green is most of luminance, so an eighth too much
                // of it is both too bright and too olive at once.
                //
                // So the three stops are set from the reference's measured
                // channel ratios at each end of its range — G/R 0.67/0.60/0.64
                // and B/R 0.44/0.32/0.36 for shadow, median and highlight — at
                // half the luminance they had.
                //
                // ## Re-measured once the specular stopped diluting them
                //
                // Half the light leaving this soil used to be specular, which is
                // the light's colour rather than the material's — so the palette
                // was being authored against a render that was washing it out,
                // and no edit here could reach the target. With the specular at
                // what a porous aggregate actually returns, these are calibrated
                // against the render one last time: measured median G/R 0.596
                // and B/R 0.317, against the reference photograph's 0.596 and
                // 0.317.
                low:  (0.00414, 0.00292, 0.00249),
                mid:  (0.03558, 0.02268, 0.01563),
                high: (0.12442, 0.08444, 0.06159),
            ),
            // A third of the dry value, and distinctly warmer: wet ground keeps
            // its red far better than its blue. Roughness is the important one
            // though — the sheen arrives before the darkening is noticeable, and
            // darkening alone is what makes wet ground read as ground in shadow.
            wet: (
                // Neutral, glossy and slumped — the same three moves the meadow
                // floor makes, for the reasons written out there. A track that
                // has been rained on is the wettest-looking ground in any
                // photograph of a field, because there is no canopy over it to
                // hide the sheen.
                // ## And it was near-black
                //
                // A fifth of the dry mid, and neutral with it. Rendered, the wet
                // stripes on the comparison card came out as dark grey panels
                // with white speckle on them — ground in deep shadow, which is
                // exactly the reading `WetResponse`'s own documentation warns
                // about, arrived at by overshooting rather than by omission.
                //
                // Wet earth is *darker and browner*, not darker and greyer. It
                // keeps its red — a water film absorbs blue hardest — so the
                // hue moves the other way from neutral. Set to 45 per cent of
                // the dry mid at G/R 0.58 and B/R 0.28: unmistakably wet beside
                // its dry stripe, and unmistakably still soil.
                wet_mid: (0.01650, 0.00957, 0.00462),
                // ## Wet mud is not a mirror
                //
                // 0.12 is a varnish, and combined with an uncapped coat it is
                // what made every wet stripe on the comparison card read as
                // poured resin. Saturated exposed mud measures nearer a third:
                // the water fills the pores between the grains, which is a large
                // change, and stops there — the surface it leaves is still
                // broken by protruding grit, floating debris and the edges of
                // its own menisci.
                //
                // Now that this scales the dry variation rather than replacing
                // it, the number is a target for the *midpoint* and the surface
                // keeps its spread either side.
                // ## Raised again, because wet mud still glinted
                //
                // At 0.28 the track rendered with a hard white catch on every
                // proud lump — small, bright, and the single most artificial
                // thing in the picture. The reference has none: wet earth is
                // *broadly* sheeny, and a narrow specular lobe on a convex bump
                // is what varnished plastic does.
                //
                // Saturated mud is a rough dark dielectric. The gloss it does
                // have belongs to a water film, which is a separate capped coat
                // layer over the top of this and only appears where one forms.
                // ## Raised a third time, and this is the one that mattered
                //
                // Saturated exposed mud is quoted around a third in the
                // literature, and a third is what a *base under a strong
                // clearcoat* should be — the coat carries the sheen and the base
                // carries the colour. With the coat cut back to what a real
                // broken film can cover, the base has to carry the wet response
                // on its own, and at 0.42 it was still glossy enough to read as
                // a moulded surface.
                //
                // Better than half. Against a dry range of 0.82 to 0.96 that is
                // still an unmistakable change — wet ground is visibly smoother
                // than dry — without ever becoming a mirror.
                roughness_wet: 0.58,
                saturation_flattening: 0.42,
            ),
            roughness_dry: (0.82, 0.96),
            ior: 1.48,

            // ## Cut back hard, because this was drawing the clods
            //
            // Region 0.5 at two metres and patch 0.9 at twenty-five centimetres
            // is an enormous amount of colour variation, and it was there
            // because it was the only variation the material had — the mesh
            // carried one shallow band and cast no shadows, so every scrap of
            // structure in a render came out of these two numbers. What they
            // actually produce at that strength is soft blotches a hand across:
            // camouflage.
            //
            // The reference plate is close to uniform in hue and its brightness
            // range comes from lit tops against shadowed sides. So the tonal
            // field goes back to being what it is for — this stretch of the
            // track is a little richer than that one — and the mesh does the
            // work.
            // ## And these were cut too far with it
            //
            // 0.5 and 0.9 were painting the clods, so they came down to 0.30 and
            // 0.34. That was right about the clods and wrong about the tonal
            // sweep: the reference photograph keeps more than half its variance
            // between nine and thirty-five centimetres, and a good part of that
            // is genuinely *colour* — richer where the ground is undisturbed,
            // scuffed pale where it has been walked, darker where it holds damp.
            //
            // Raised to where the broad term does that work and the fine one
            // stays out of the clod band, which is the split that matters. The
            // region wavelength is what carries it now rather than the patch.
            region_wavelength_m: 1.6,
            region_strength: 0.52,
            patch_wavelength_m: 0.22,
            patch_strength: 0.30,
            grain_strength: 0.55,
        ),

        // ## Measured against the reference, and it was flat
        //
        // The band list here used to be three entries of which the mesh carried
        // **one**: a five-centimetre clod at seventeen millimetres. Exported and
        // measured off `ground.bin`, bare soil came back at 2.0 mm peak-to-peak
        // with a mean slope of 5.6 degrees and a maximum of 16.3. The sun is at
        // 35, so nothing on the ground could shadow anything else, anywhere, at
        // any moisture — and the whole apparent structure of a soil render was
        // pigment standing in for form.
        //
        // `docs/references/grass_to_mud_bumpy.jpg`, measured over a 760-square
        // crop of its bare ground, puts **32 per cent of its variance between
        // three and nine centimetres** and runs fifteen to one from its dark
        // decile to its bright one. That is clods with lit tops and shadowed
        // sides. No colour field reaches that range, which is why chasing it in
        // the palette produced a card with camouflage painted on it.
        //
        // So: five bands, three of them on the mesh, declared coarsest first.
        // The slopes add, which is how real ground gets faces steeper than any
        // one of its scales.
        structure: (
            bands: [
                // The swell of turned earth. Broad, shallow, and the band that
                // owns the reference's dominant nine-to-thirty-five centimetre
                // variance. Barely touched by compaction: rolling a field flat
                // does not remove its shape at this scale, it removes the crumb.
                (wavelength_m: 0.1500, amplitude_m: 0.0280, shape: "Rounded", compaction_response: 0.30, clustered: false),
                // **The clods.** Six centimetres across, standing a good third
                // of that, and shaped so the sides are flanks rather than
                // gradients — see `AggregateShape::profile`. This is the band
                // the reference is mostly made of and the only one that has ever
                // needed to cast a shadow.
                // ## Angular, because a clod is broken and not moulded
                //
                // `RoundedRidged` all through this band's life, and the render
                // kept coming back described as procedurally rounded — dimples
                // in clay rather than soil. Looking at the reference again that
                // is the plainest difference left: its clods have **broken,
                // planar faces**. They were torn apart, and material that is
                // torn parts along flats and edges rather than settling into
                // domes.
                //
                // `Angular` narrows the wall between levels from 0.34 of the
                // field's range to 0.14 — see `AggregateShape::profile` — which
                // is a face rather than a shoulder.
                (wavelength_m: 0.0600, amplitude_m: 0.0280, shape: "Angular", compaction_response: 0.30, clustered: true),
                // Crumb riding on the clods. Still geometry — it is what stops
                // the clods reading as poured concrete lumps — and the finest
                // thing this soil asks the mesh for.
                (wavelength_m: 0.0160, amplitude_m: 0.0045, shape: "RoundedRidged", compaction_response: 0.35, clustered: true),
                // Grit, into the shader as a bump.
                (wavelength_m: 0.0060, amplitude_m: 0.0022, compaction_response: 0.45, clustered: true),
                // Grain. Barely flattened by compaction — a wheel presses clods
                // down and hardly touches what is between them.
                //
                // The whole ladder of responses was steeper than this once, and
                // a worn track came back looking like paper. Packing a soil
                // takes maybe two thirds of its coarse relief away, not five
                // sixths: a path that has been walked on for a year still has
                // clods, they are just flatter than the ones beside it.
                (wavelength_m: 0.0012, amplitude_m: 0.0006, compaction_response: 0.20),
            ],
            cluster_wavelength_m: 0.8,
            // Half, which is where it started. Lowering it to 0.38 was chasing
            // the wrong shortage: the ground was not short of clods, it was
            // short of *floor between* them — see `AggregateShape::profile`,
            // which is where that got fixed. With clods properly sparse the
            // clustering goes back to doing its own job, which is giving a track
            // calm stretches beside broken ones.
            cluster_strength: 0.5,
            // ## This buys sharpness, not just another band
            //
            // Sixteen millimetres puts the lattice at four, and the third band
            // is the only one that actually needs carrying at that scale. The
            // rest of what it buys is the **clod flank**: `shape` widens a flank
            // the lattice cannot resolve, and at a five-and-a-half millimetre
            // step the six-centimetre band's declared flank of 0.34 was being
            // opened to 0.46 — a third of its steepness given away to sampling.
            // At four it binds, and the clods have edges.
            //
            // Costs about two and a quarter million vertices on a six-metre
            // plate, which is why the mesh is built through the buffer API now.
            mesh_floor_m: Some(0.0160),
            cohesion: 0.62,
        ),

        // A packed path does craze when it dries right out, and the ceiling is
        // low enough that a track with any moisture in it does not.
        cracks: Some((
            // ## Seen for the first time on a bone-dry card, and too strong
            //
            // No shipped document had ever driven this soil below a moisture of
            // about 0.35, so the network had never opened past a fraction of
            // itself. `wet_and_dry` runs its dry stripes at zero, and what came
            // out was crazy paving: even polygons, even widths, the whole
            // surface tiled.
            //
            // Real desiccation is a *hierarchy* — a few wide primaries with
            // finer branches dying against them — and it covers a fraction of
            // the ground rather than all of it. Wider polygons and a much
            // weaker secondary give that; the depth stays, because the depth is
            // what catches the light.
            polygon_m: 0.22,
            width_m: 0.0032,
            depth_m: 0.012,
            secondary: 0.14,
            curl_m: 0.0008,
            moisture_ceiling: 0.25,
        )),

        scatter: (
            // ## The reference's texture *is* the grit
            //
            // Ninety a square metre. Shown a crop of the reference photograph
            // beside a crop of the render at the same magnification, the gap is
            // not the shading and it is not the heightfield — it is that the
            // photograph is made of **thousands of discrete small objects**,
            // each with a lit top and a shadowed side, and the render is a
            // smooth surface with holes in it.
            //
            // That is a representation difference and no amount of simulating
            // the heightfield can close it. A height field is by definition a
            // single continuous surface; soil at this scale is a heap of
            // separate fragments lying on one. Ninety a square metre is one
            // every ten centimetres — invisible. Two and a half thousand is one
            // every two, which is what a photograph of a track shows.
            //
            // Radii go down as well as up: most of what covers real ground is
            // crumb a couple of millimetres across, with the occasional larger
            // lump. The old floor of four millimetres had nothing below it at
            // all.
            // ## Sized to what the frame can resolve, not to what soil has
            //
            // A millimetre and a half to fourteen is honest about real crumb and
            // renders as nothing: at this project's trace resolution a five
            // millimetre fragment covers **a pixel and a half**, so ninety
            // thousand of them arrived as a faint grey haze over a smooth
            // surface. Seen in silhouette against the black at the plate's edge
            // the same fragments read perfectly — crisp, lit, distinctly
            // separate objects — which is what says the geometry is right and
            // the size is not.
            //
            // Ten to thirty-six millimetres across puts a fragment at three to
            // twelve pixels, which is the band where a thing has a lit top and a
            // shadow beside it. Fewer of them, because they are each several
            // times the area: a quarter of the ground covered rather than all of
            // it.
            //
            // The crumb below this scale has not gone anywhere. It is in the
            // shader's bump and then in the roughness, which is the same
            // three-tier split the relief uses and for the same reason.
            // ## The clods take the shadowing budget from the pocks
            //
            // See `docs/todo/soil-perforation.md`. Four separate attempts to
            // reduce the surface's uniform perforation each dropped the
            // self-shadowing share below its floor, because in the simulated bed
            // the relief that reads as perforation *is* the relief that casts.
            //
            // The way out is a second source of steep relief that is not a hole.
            // These fragments are instanced geometry sitting on the bed rather
            // than part of it, so they can carry the shadows while the bed goes
            // quiet — which is the trade the reference actually shows: a fairly
            // calm surface with distinct clods standing on it.
            //
            // Paired with the disturbance rate coming down in `soil.rs`. Tuning
            // either alone moves the picture sideways, which is exactly what the
            // four failed attempts did.
            grit_per_m2: 380.0,
            pebble_per_m2: 46.0,
            fragment_radius_m: (0.009, 0.030),
        ),

        vegetation_affinity: 0.0,
    ),
)
